From b51e12f091000a62ae78e451062f249443c2f583 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 06:28:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] AGENTS.md, tests/test_boundaries.py: nci/ and rho/ are no
 longer under verity/protocols/inference_only/

The move took the two calculators to benchmarks/. AGENTS.md's map of verity/protocols/ listed them as code a move
brought from benchmarks/, which the move's rewrite left there under their new paths, and SEPARATE still named their
old directories. No test names either line.

Co-authored-by: danielreuter <danielreuter@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 AGENTS.md                | 3 +--
 tests/test_boundaries.py | 3 +--
 2 files changed, 2 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/AGENTS.md b/AGENTS.md
index 3d4a2f19cb5..8d30d8694cf 100644
--- a/AGENTS.md
+++ b/AGENTS.md
@@ -27,4 +27,3 @@ it; a line marked "Target, not yet" says what the layout of record has and the t
     interface each protocol's check implements. Code the move brought from `benchmarks/`, which no unit rule reads yet:
-    `pouw/clear/`, `pouw/receipts/`, `poub/device/live_verifier/`, `poub/verifier/remote_session/`,
-    `benchmarks/nci/` and `benchmarks/rho/`.
+    `pouw/clear/`, `pouw/receipts/`, `poub/device/live_verifier/` and `poub/verifier/remote_session/`.
   - `verity/circuits/`: the circuit language (format in `circuits/PROTOCOL.md`). Three base Lean packages, which Flock's
diff --git a/tests/test_boundaries.py b/tests/test_boundaries.py
index 9638fe72c0e..72cab8fc519 100644
--- a/tests/test_boundaries.py
+++ b/tests/test_boundaries.py
@@ -214,4 +214,3 @@ DEPENDENCIES = {"numpy"}
 SEPARATE = ("protocols/flock/agreement", "protocols/flock/pod", "protocols/flock/prover", "protocols/flock/tool.py",
-            "difftest", "protocols/pous/verifier/check", "configuration/config", "protocols/inference_only/rho",
-            "protocols/inference_only/nci")
+            "difftest", "protocols/pous/verifier/check", "configuration/config")
 #: Source subtrees that must stay stdlib-only, the scalar references, and within them what may use numpy.
